**Depth-First Search (DFS)** takes the opposite strategy from BFS. Instead of exploring all neighbours before going deeper, DFS commits to one path as deeply as possible — following edges until it reaches a dead end, then backtracking to explore other branches.

DFS can be implemented **recursively** (using the call stack implicitly) or **iteratively** (using an explicit stack). The recursive version is more elegant for many applications:

```
function dfs(graph, v, visited) {
  visited.add(v);
  process(v);
  for (const neighbour of graph.neighbours(v)) {
    if (!visited.has(neighbour)) dfs(graph, neighbour, visited);
  }
}
```

The iterative version is important when graphs are large enough to overflow the call stack (JavaScript's default stack depth is around 10,000–15,000 frames).

**Time and space complexity:** Identical to BFS — **O(V + E)** time, O(V) space for the visited set and stack.

DFS does **not** guarantee shortest paths. The path DFS finds to a vertex depends on the order edges are stored in the adjacency list — DFS might find a long winding path when a shorter one exists. This is a crucial difference to remember.

**What DFS is good at:** Topological sorting of DAGs; cycle detection in both directed and undirected graphs; finding strongly connected components (Tarjan's or Kosaraju's algorithm); solving maze puzzles (finding any path, not necessarily shortest); detecting back edges; computing finish times for scheduling; generating spanning trees; determining if a graph is biconnected.

A key concept in DFS is the **DFS tree** — the tree formed by the edges DFS actually follows. Edges in the original graph that are not in the DFS tree are classified as: **back edges** (to an ancestor — indicates a cycle in directed graphs), **forward edges** (to a descendant not yet in the tree), and **cross edges** (to a vertex in a different DFS subtree). In undirected graphs, DFS only produces tree edges and back edges — a back edge in an undirected DFS always means a cycle.

